# 6. Custom ORM — Odoo-inspired, TypeScript-native

Build RIOTWORKS' own modular ORM in packages/orm. Its design may learn from Odoo's metadata-driven models, model registry, addon manifests, dependency ordering, and model extension approach.

Do not copy Odoo source code or claim API compatibility. Implement an idiomatic TypeScript architecture.

The ORM must be metadata-driven and addon-aware.

## 6.1 Required ORM capabilities

Core:
- Persistent, abstract and transient model kinds.
- Model/addon registry, typed fields, constraints, defaults and indexes.
- Deterministic extension/inheritance and explicit composition.
- Computed, related, translated and relational fields.
- Domain/query AST, typed recordsets, batch operations and safe query compilation.
- Request-scoped identity map, prefetch cache and invalidation.
- Lifecycle hooks, environment/context, unit-of-work and concurrency integration.
- Model ACL, record rules, field policies and workspace/company scope integration.
- Programmatic schema reconciliation, typed data-upgrade coordination and adapter capability checks.
- Normalized errors, telemetry and cross-provider contracts.

Initial field types:
- string
- text
- boolean
- integer
- float
- decimal (preserve precision; never silently treat money as binary float)
- date
- datetime
- JSON
- enum/selection
- UUID/identifier
- relation: many-to-one, one-to-many, many-to-many where adapter supports it

Query API:
- findById
- findOne
- search
- count
- create
- createMany only when supported
- update
- updateMany only when supported
- archive / restore
- delete only when policy permits
- pagination, sort, projection, and filter/domain expressions

Query operators must be validated against field metadata and adapter capability. Never build query strings by concatenating untrusted user input. Unsupported operations must fail explicitly rather than silently changing semantics.

## 6.2 Declarative model example

Implement a clean TypeScript declaration style, for example:

    export const Project = defineModel({
      name: "project.project",
      label: "Project",
      table: "projects",
      fields: {
        name: fields.string({ required: true, maxLength: 160 }),
        description: fields.text({ nullable: true }),
        state: fields.selection({
          values: ["draft", "active", "completed", "cancelled"],
          default: "draft"
        }),
        startAt: fields.datetime({ nullable: true }),
        ownerId: fields.relation.belongsTo("base.user", {
          nullable: true
        })
      },
      indexes: [
        index("project_name_idx", ["name"])
      ]
    });

This is illustrative. Design actual types and builders so invalid combinations are caught at compile time and validated at runtime.

## 6.3 Model extension

Support:
- New models.
- Extension of an existing model through addon declarations.
- Field addition with conflict checks.
- Method/service extension with deterministic ordering.
- Explicit dependency declarations.
- Stable registry composition.
- Conflict errors for duplicate field names, incompatible types, circular dependencies, and ambiguous overrides.

Do not use uncontrolled runtime monkey-patching, arbitrary eval, or arbitrary code loading from untrusted addons.

## 6.4 ORM environment

Provide a scoped execution environment containing:
- Adapter/database context.
- Workspace/tenant context where enabled.
- Actor/user identity.
- Permission context.
- Locale/timezone where relevant.
- Request/trace ID.
- Transaction capability or transaction handle when available.
- Unit-of-work scope.

Do not keep mutable per-request user context in global singletons.

# 6A. Programmatic schema management — mandatory architecture

Desired schema source: trusted TypeScript addon models composed by ORM registry. Execution owner: packages/orm-db. Physical operations: selected SQL adapter. Trigger: private ARSY operations API and same service exposed to CLI/CI/deployment.

No hand-authored schema migration files for ordinary model changes. No routine migration directory, generator, or migration-runner workflow.

Private ARSY API:
- POST /internal/admin/schema/plan
- POST /internal/admin/schema/reconcile
- POST /internal/admin/schema/verify
- GET /internal/admin/schema/status

Private control plane only. Never accept arbitrary SQL, user schema, or executable code. CLI, CI, release job, and HTTP invoke the same SchemaReconciliationService.

The reconciler validates trusted addons, composes models/BaseEntity/mixins, introspects actual schema/capabilities, creates deterministic plan, classifies operations, applies permitted safe changes, verifies results, records receipts.

Adapter uses its DB connection and provider dialect/schema builder to apply physical DDL. SQLite/libSQL/PostgreSQL do not expose one universal REST schema endpoint. ARSY supplies internal API; adapter executes native schema operations. This removes handwritten files, not physical DDL.

Modes: plan (read-only), safe (compatible additive changes), strict (fail on drift), off (no writes). Local may use safe; CI runs plan; production uses one serialized release job then strict verification. Never let every ARSY replica mutate schema.

SAFE_AUTO: create registered table; add nullable field; add deterministic portable default; add compatible index after uniqueness validation; add optional relation/constraint.
REQUIRES_DATA_UPGRADE: rename preserving data, type/precision conversion, nullable-to-required backfill, enum remap, split/merge, semantic transformation. Use typed TS handlers with preconditions, idempotency, bounded batches, checkpoints, postconditions.
DESTRUCTIVE: drop field/table/index, truncate/overwrite, narrowing with possible data loss, risky cascade. Never unattended; require approval, verified backup, impact report, recovery plan.
UNSUPPORTED: typed capability error; never silently emulate.

System schema ledger records desired/actual fingerprints, addon versions, plan hashes, actor, environment, operation receipts, checkpoints, and errors. It is not migration-file storage. Reconciler owns only RIOTWORKS-managed objects.

# 8. Addon system — Odoo-inspired module architecture

An addon is trusted versioned code declaring technical ID/name, version, platform/ORM compatibility, dependencies, model declarations/extensions, services/hooks, permissions, optional typed data-upgrade handlers, and route/integration registrations.

Do not declare routine schema migration lists in manifests. Desired schema derives from model metadata. Typed data-upgrade handlers exist only for semantic data transformations.

Lifecycle: DISCOVER → VALIDATE → RESOLVE → COMPOSE → PLAN → SAFE RECONCILE → DATA UPGRADE IF REQUIRED → VERIFY → INITIALIZE → READY.

Resolve deterministically. Reject missing/circular dependencies, duplicate IDs, model/field conflicts, incompatible versions, and untrusted code. Disable/uninstall never silently deletes data. Never auto-execute arbitrary community addon code.

# 9. Base platform — Odoo-inspired foundation suite

Do not confuse the minimal BaseEntity record contract with the complete base platform. BaseEntity is composed once by the ORM. The complete addon catalog, ownership matrix and feature target are defined in docs/22.

## 9.1 Universal BaseEntity

Every persisted model receives exactly five ORM-managed fields:
- id: opaque stable string identifier.
- createdAt: immutable UTC creation instant.
- createdById: nullable reference to a canonical principal, set from trusted execution context.
- updatedAt: UTC instant of last successful persisted mutation.
- updatedById: nullable reference to a canonical principal, set from trusted execution context.

Null actors are allowed only for documented bootstrap/system/import provenance. Never trust actor IDs supplied directly by clients. Addons never redeclare these fields.

Do not inject active, archivedAt, deletedAt, version, workspaceId, companyId, displayName, or audit payload universally. They have distinct semantics and belong to explicit mixins or owning addons.

## 9.2 Base platform addon suite

Keep packages/orm-addon-base as a release package but split it internally into independently declared addon manifests:
- base.core
- base.identity
- base.security
- base.workspace
- base.company (optional)
- base.audit
- base.events
- base.communication (optional)
- base.activity (optional)
- base.attachment
- base.sequence
- base.settings
- base.localization
- base.transient
- base.import-export
- base.integration (optional)
- base.taxonomy (optional)

The addons own separate models/services and dependencies. Do not create one giant base model or one monolithic addon. docs/22 defines exact ownership and dependencies.

## 9.3 Reusable mixins

OwnershipAttribution, Archiveable, SoftDeletable, Toggleable, Versioned, WorkspaceScoped, CompanyScoped, ParentTree, ChatterEnabled, ActivityEnabled, AttachmentEnabled, SequenceAssigned, Translatable and CustomMetadata are explicit opt-in behavior bundles. Mixins define fields, policy, query semantics and lifecycle behavior; they are not global field bags.

## 9.4 Strict ownership boundaries

AuditEvent is generic change history. DomainEvent/Outbox is reliable business event delivery. Message/Thread is collaboration history. Activity is a human follow-up. Background Job is executable worker work. Notification is recipient delivery. Do not merge them into a catch-all table.

Base platform addons own identity, security, workspace, company and shared services. Business addons own Project, Product, Opportunity, Asset, Finance and AI/Agent models. See docs/22.

